<script>
  import BlocksDemo from '$lib/demos/BlocksDemo.svelte';
</script>

A **block** page is data: a tree of nodes. Each node names a component `type` plus its `props`. `blocks()` renders that tree the same way that `markdown()` renders `.svx`: as an entry `body` that you drop in with `<Region of={entry.body} />`. It is the render half of a visual page builder. Your CMS (or a JSON file) owns the layout. ogygia owns the rendering.

::: tip When to use
Use blocks when *content decides layout*: marketing pages assembled in a visual editor, CMS-driven landing pages, each case where a non-developer arranges components. When a developer writes the layout in a `.svelte` file, you do not need this. Only write the components.
:::

The panel below is a block tree (a `Hero`, a `Grid` of two `Feature`s, and one interactive `CounterBlock`) rendered live from the data lower on this page.

<div class="doc-demo">
  <BlocksDemo />
</div>

## Register your blocks

The registry maps a block `type` name to a component. A **static** block is a `with { region: 'raw' }` import: it renders inline and ships zero JS. But it is code-split. Thus a page loads only the CSS (and, for interactive blocks, the JS) of the block types that it names. An **interactive** block uses `with { wake: … }` in its place. That bakes its schedule, and makes its JS **load only when a page names it** (`load` · `idle` · `visible` · a media query decides when it hydrates).

```ts title=src/lib/blocks.ts
// a .ts module: a `region`/`wake` mark here makes a held block
import Hero    from './blocks/Hero.svelte'    with { region: 'raw' };
import Grid    from './blocks/Grid.svelte'    with { region: 'raw' };
import Feature from './blocks/Feature.svelte' with { region: 'raw' };
import Counter from './blocks/Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };

export const registry = { Hero, Grid, Feature, Counter };
```

Both marks make the component a held block: code-split, resolved to a region by the registry. `region: 'raw'` bakes no schedule (static, HTML only). `wake:` bakes one (interactive). A plain import would operate too. But its CSS would fold into the page stylesheet. Then a large registry pays for each block on each page. The marks are what keep the cost of a page to the blocks that it renders.

> The `with { region: 'raw' }` lines are N imports that quietly fall out of sync with the folder. The **[`regions()` macro](/docs/macros/regions)** collapses them into one line, `import.meta.og.regions('./blocks/*.svelte')`. It globs the folder and emits a raw-region import per match, keyed by basename. Blocks that need a `wake:` schedule stay a manual import, spread on top.

You decide which component a `type` means. There is no guessing and no auto-discovery. An unregistered `type` is skipped, with a dev warning. It is never rendered as something that it is not.

## A page is JSON

```json
[
  { "type": "Hero", "props": { "title": "Welcome" } },
  { "type": "Grid", "children": [
    { "type": "Feature", "props": { "title": "Fast" } },
    { "type": "Feature", "props": { "title": "Typed" } }
  ] },
  { "type": "Counter", "props": { "start": 3 } }
]
```

Nodes nest through `children`: a `Grid` holds `Feature`s, a section holds a grid. That is what makes it a page tree, and not a flat stack.

## Render it

`blocks()` is a content source. Point a collection at your page JSON, and the `body` of each entry is the rendered tree:

```ts
import { content, blocks } from 'ogygia/content';
import { registry } from '$lib/blocks';

export const pages = content({
  loader: blocks(import.meta.glob('./pages/*.json'), registry)
});
```

Then render it exactly like a markdown body: the same `get()`, the same `<Region>`. Call `get()` in a **universal** `+page.ts` load, or directly in the page component with `await`. Its data reaches the render by reference, in the same server pass.

```ts title=+page.ts
// universal, NOT +page.server.ts
import { pages } from '$lib/blocks/pages';
export const load = async () => ({ page: await pages.get('landing') });
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<Region of={data.page.body} />
```

::: warning Not `+page.server.ts`
The data of a server load crosses a serialization boundary. A `body` is a same-pass SSR value: a live render, not wire data. Thus it cannot cross. ogygia stops you, with an error that says exactly this. On a `csr = false` page, a universal load never runs client-side. Thus it is server code, with none of the boundary.
:::

The **CSS of each block travels with the render**. The page never imports `Hero` or `Feature`. Thus their styles are on no page stylesheet. During the server pass, the region links the hashed stylesheet of each rendered block into `<head>`, deduped, and only for the block types that the page uses. A registry of 100 blocks costs a page exactly the sheets for the blocks that it renders.

## No collection? A ten-line recipe

The content source is the recommended path. But when you already hold a tree (a prop, a literal, a one-off CMS call), you do not need a collection. `blocks.resolve(tree, registry)` turns it into region nodes. It must run **server-side**, where the signing key lives: a `+page.server.ts` load, a remote, or the SSR pass of a `csr = false` page. You render those nodes with a small recomposer that you own:

```svelte title=BlockTree.svelte
<!-- yours to keep and tweak -->
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  import { blocks } from 'ogygia/content';
  import Self from './BlockTree.svelte';

  let { tree, registry, nodes } = $props();
  const resolved = $derived(nodes ?? blocks.resolve(tree, registry));
</script>

{#each resolved as node, i (i)}
  <Region of={node.of}>{#if node.children?.length}<Self nodes={node.children} />{/if}</Region>
{/each}
```

```svelte
<!-- use it -->
<BlockTree tree={page} {registry} />
```

That is the full thing. `blocks.resolve` does the `type → region` walk, and skips unknown types. Your component does the rendering. It is exactly what the `blocks()` source runs internally. There is no shipped component to import, because ten lines that you own beat a black box.

## Data from a CMS (Builder.io)

Builder is headless: it serves your pages as JSON over an HTTP API. Write a **loader** that fetches it, and render through `blocks()`. Copy this, drop in your public key, and you have a Builder-driven site:

```ts title=src/lib/builder.ts
import { content, blocks, mapRaw } from 'ogygia/content';
import type { RawSource } from 'ogygia/content';
import { registry } from './blocks';

const KEY = 'YOUR_BUILDER_PUBLIC_KEY';
const API = `https://cdn.builder.io/api/v3/content/page?apiKey=${KEY}`;

// Builder element → block node.
const toTree = (els = []) =>
  els.filter((e) => e.component?.name).map((e) => ({
    type: e.component.name,
    props: e.component.options ?? {},
    children: e.children ? toTree(e.children) : undefined
  }));

// A raw source over Builder's Content API: refs() lists every page, get() fetches ONE.
const builderApi: RawSource<any> = {
  async refs() {
    const { results } = await fetch(`${API}&limit=100`).then((r) => r.json());
    return results.map((r) => ({ id: (r.data?.url ?? r.id).replace(/^\//, ''), value: r }));
  },
  async get(id) {
    const { results } = await fetch(`${API}&limit=1&url=/${id}`).then((r) => r.json());
    return results[0] ? { id, value: results[0] } : null;
  }
};

// Convert each result to a block tree, then render through blocks().
export const pages = content({
  loader: blocks(mapRaw(builderApi, (r) => ({ blocks: toTree(r.data?.blocks) })), registry)
});
```

That is the full integration. `get(slug)` fetches one page on demand. `list()` / `ids()` drive a nav or prerender. Nothing else in your app changes. It is a content collection, the same as all others.

The live demo on this site uses local JSON in the exact shape of Builder: **[/demo/builder/home](/demo/builder/home)** and **[/demo/builder/pricing](/demo/builder/pricing)**. Full pages, assembled as data, rendered as islands. Only the interactive block ships JS.

## Thousands of blocks, only a few shipped

The registry can hold a thousand blocks. A page that names three loads three chunks. The other 997 never ship. That is not a special case. It falls out of the model. The registry is fixed at build (the vocabulary). The page JSON picks the arrangement at runtime. Each block is a `held region`. The client chunk of a held region loads only when it is on the page. Thus the bundle stays small, however large the registry grows.
